{
  "case": "fix the median bug",
  "variant": "without",
  "trial": 0,
  "run": {
    "final_text": "I fixed the bug in `median()`, and all 3 tests now pass.\n\n**The bug:** `median()` always returned the single middle element. That's only correct for an odd number of values. With an even number there are two middle values, so it was returning the upper one. For example, `[1, 2, 3, 4]` gave `3` instead of `2.5`.\n\n**The fix** in `stats.py`: when the list has an even length, it now returns the average of the two middle values:\n\n```python\ndef median(values):\n    ordered = sorted(values)\n    middle = len(ordered) // 2\n    if len(ordered) % 2 == 0:\n        return (ordered[middle - 1] + ordered[middle]) / 2\n    return ordered[middle]\n```\n\npytest isn't installed here, so I ran the tests with `python -m unittest`.\n\nOne thing I left alone: `mean()` and `median()` still crash on an empty list. The tests don't cover that case.",
